\begin_layout Standard
Since your OpenOCD setup is already working smoothly with the Pico 2W,
 your debug probe configuration is fully functional.
 [1] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To transition from standard sequential debugging to monitoring your dual-core Ada application across both ARM Cortex-M33 processors,
 you can leverage GDB’s built-in multi-threading mechanisms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
## 1.
 Identify Your Cores in GDB
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because your light_tasking_rp2350 profile is actively scheduling operations across both CPUs,
 OpenOCD maps physical Core 0 and Core 1 as separate execution threads.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once your application hits a breakpoint or you interrupt execution (using Ctrl+C),
 run this command inside the (gdb) prompt to see exactly what each core is doing:
 [2] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
info threads
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Example Output:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Id Target Id Frame
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* 1 Thread 1 (rp2350.core0) main () at src/main.adb:6
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2 Thread 2 (rp2350.core1) <idling> at 0x10000240
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The asterisk (*) indicates which core your terminal context is currently inspecting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
## 2.
 Switch Between the ARM Cores
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To shift your debugging focus (for example,
 to inspect variables or step through logic executing exclusively on Core 1),
 swap your thread context:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
# Switch execution focus to Core 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
thread 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
# Inspect local variables active on Core 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
info locals
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
# Step forward one line on Core 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
next
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
## 3.
 Apply Multi-Core Hardware Breakpoints
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When debugging real-time blinking loops on separate processors,
 a breakpoint hit on one core will freeze execution globally across both cores.
 You can restrict breakpoints to trigger only when hit by a specific CPU core using the thread ID:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
# Set a breakpoint on line 12,
 but ONLY trigger if Core 1 hits it
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
break main.adb:12 thread 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
## 4.
 Direct Register Verification
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you ever want to verify with absolute certainty which hardware core your active GDB context is stepping through,
 read the RP2350's SIO hardware CPUID register directly from memory:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
# Directly query the hardware register address 0xd0000000
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
print /x *0xd0000000
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* If GDB returns 0x0,
 your terminal context is actively bound to Core 0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* If GDB returns 0x1,
 your terminal context is actively bound to Core 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Are your tasks communicating with each other using Ada protected objects,
 or would you like to see how to debug the hardware inter-core FIFO registers directly through GDB?
\end_layout

